{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1549384-armor-spirit-charm","id":1549384,"title":"Armor (spirit charm)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-03-17T06:14:43-07:00","id":1549384,"title":"Armor (spirit charm)","content":"Folks, sorry if this has been answered before (I couldn't find anything by searching, though), but it's been a long time since I've run a Werewolf game, and while re-reading the books lately, a very silly question came up: the Armor charm adds a spirit's Gnosis to its Willpower when soaking damage, right?<br /> <br /> Because the charm's description seems to indicate that it replaces Willpower instead:<br /> <br /> <i>This Charm grants a spirit a measure of protection. By expending two points of Essence, the spirit gains a soak pool equal to its Gnosis for the remainder of the scene</i>. [Core book, revised, pg. 238]<br /> <br /> And the description in the W20 Core Book is exactly the same: <i>By spending two points of Essence, the spirit gains a soak pool equal to its Gnosis for the remainder of the scene</i>. [pg. 366]<br /> <br /> However, since some spirits have more Willpower than Gnosis (like Earth Elementals and Echoes, for instance) replacing it would make no sense. So, I guess the description should read that Armor <b>adds</b> a spirit's gnosis to its Willpower soak pool, correct?<br /> <br /> Thanks in advance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28735,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","user":"Greywolf76","date":". 03-18-2025, 05:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-17T23:18:27-07:00","id":1549402,"title":"","content":"It's very poorly worded yes. It could be phrased any number of different ways, but the Charm can be extremely useful or utterly pointless if you read it as a replacement of Willpower for Gnosis. It letting you roll Gnosis + Willpower is the only interpretation that makes sense even if not the most technical way to read the words... so go for it. &quot;Gains Gnosis soak dice,&quot; and, &quot;gains a soak pool equal to its Gnosis,&quot; are viably equivalent statements.<br /> <br /> So... go for the additive version even if a bit of a tortured way to reading the rules. Or call it a house rule. Either way it's better (well, unless the players are trying to beat a Gnosis 10/Willpower 10 spirit with the Charm).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-18T06:07:38-07:00","id":1549406,"title":"","content":"Thanks, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a>. It's amazing that editors and proofreaders overlooked that error the W20 core book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-03-18T14:34:37-07:00","id":1549417,"title":"","content":"To give some context on said folks' behalf:<br /> <br /> While the 20th core book tomes tend to have large credit pages, the actual number of people doing the core work of things like development, editing, and proofing were normal in number for a book half the content. V20 and W20, which were much more &quot;omnibus edition love letters with a few rules tweaks,&quot; also did a lot more straight up copy &amp; paste from Revised to help overcome that (M20 and onward saw much heavier revisions of the rules, leading to a lot of old mistakes not getting reprinted... but plenty of new ones also cropping up).<br /> <br /> It's also important to remember that content editing (where you catch things like this) and copy editing (which is looking for overt language and layout errors) are two separate jobs (even if in TTRPG books a lot of jobs are done by the same person or small group of people). Proof readers and most TTRPG credits for editors are copy editors. Their job isn't to look for things like, &quot;hey rule X and rule Y interact funky.&quot; Their job is to make sure things make sense to a reader and trust the developers and authors that the meaning of one paragraph lines up with the meaning of a different paragraph however many pages earlier. So a copy editor that reads &quot;<i>gains a soak pool equal to its Gnosis</i>,&quot; they have to assume that somewhere else in the book explains how to carry out the function of &quot;gain a soak pool&quot; and what defines &quot;Gnosis&quot; as a variable. That detachment from the holistic meaning of the text is important because it helps them keep focused on the details they're hired to hunt for.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons mistakes like this tend to slip through is because content editing, which most TTRPGs put on the developer(s), is that they're seen these lines dozens or hundreds of times. Just like people of fans who've been playing for years and have read the book lots of times can still gloss over a passage until someone else sits them down and makes them read it over again (happened to me recently in the big Gift threads on spending WP on Gift roll activation). It's really easy to miss that either you've been internally correcting something for years, or just you always read it one way and didn't think if it could be read fairly differently.<br /> <br /> In the end, for us as readers/players/STs it sucks, but there's really no way to 100% prevent it, there's a practical limit where trying to get closer to 100% means spending more time and energy on a product that eventually does need to sell and make a profit so you can keep making more books; even more so when you remember the W20 was one of Onyx Path's first big projects as a brand new independent company (V20 core was done while everything was still being done in-house under CCP management), and Rich has kindly given plenty of insight over the years into how Onyx Path had to adapt its practices over the years from different mistakes made in the past.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}